window.docContents[91].resolve({"/#monads-arithmetic-example-no-effects":{"contents":"The type  has no constructors, and thus no values, like the Nothing type in Scala or Kotlin.\nIn Scala and Kotlin, Nothing can represent computations that never return a result, such as functions that crash the program, throw exceptions, or always fall into infinite loops.\nAn argument to a function or method of type Nothing indicates dead code, as there will never be a suitable argument value.\nLean doesn't support infinite loops and exceptions, but  is still useful as an indication to the type system that a function cannot be called.\nUsing the syntax  when  is an expression whose type has no constructors indicates to Lean that the current expression need not return a result, because it could never have been called.\n\nUsing  as the parameter to  indicates that there are no additional cases beyond , , and , because it is impossible to come up with a value of type  to place in the  constructor.\nBecause a function to apply an operator of type  to two integers can never be called, it doesn't need to return a result.\nThus, it can be used in any monad:\n\n\n\nThis can be used together with , the identity monad, to evaluate expressions that have no effects whatsoever:\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monads\u0009Example: Arithmetic in Monads\u0009Further Effects","header":"4.3.3.1. No Effects","id":"/#monads-arithmetic-example-no-effects"},"/#out-params":{"contents":"An instance of the  class is sufficient to allow two expressions with type  to be conveniently added, producing another .\nHowever, in many cases, it can be useful to be more flexible and allow heterogeneous operator overloading, where the arguments may have different types.\nFor example, adding a  to a  or a  to a  will always yield a :\n\n\n\nThese functions allow natural numbers to be added to positive numbers, but they cannot be used with the  type class, which expects both arguments to  to have the same type.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes","header":"3.3. Controlling Instance Search","id":"/#out-params"}});